<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matsuo Basho</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1644">1644</a>–<a href="/years/1694">1694</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matsuo Basho was a Japanese poet of the seventeenth century, regarded as the greatest master of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiku">haiku</a>. His works, including the travel journal The Narrow Road to the Deep North, combined poetry and prose. He is among the cultural figures of the peaceful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a> era.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matsuo-basho.jpg" alt="Matsuo Basho" width="252" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Morikawa Kyoriku (1656-1715) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basho_by_Morikawa_Kyoriku_(1656-1715).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The arts flourished under the Tokugawa peace: kabuki and bunraku puppet theatre, the ukiyo-e woodblock prints of artists such as Hokusai and Hiroshige, the hokku verse, ancestor of the haiku, that <b>Matsuo Basho</b> raised to a high art, and an urban popular culture centred on the chonin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bashō's birth house, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Midōsuji. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.77,136.14&amp;year=1676" aria-label="Bashō's birth house on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8005 905 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Matsuo Basho"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8455 1175h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8455 1175h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.77,136.14&amp;year=1676">What was here in 1676? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
